1. Respiratory
	•	Community-Acquired Pneumonia (CAP):
	•	Epidemiology & Burden: Local incidence, high HIV/TB co-infection burden, and mortality figures.
	•	Etiology & Pathogens: Common global and local bacterial/viral causes (Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis, Staphylococcus aureus, Klebsiella pneumoniae, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, respiratory viruses).
	•	Endemic & Opportunistic Pathogens: Mycobacterium tuberculosis presenting as acute pneumonia, Pneumocystis jirovecii (PCP), and Cryptococcus neoformans in HIV-positive patients.
	•	Clinical & Radiological Diagnosis: Symptoms vs. physical signs, lobar vs. interstitial infiltrates on CXR, and the significance of air bronchograms.
	•	Risk Stratification & Severity Scoring: CURB-65 scoring system, indications for hospitalization, and ICU admission criteria.
	•	Biomarkers & Microbiology: Role and limitations of CRP, procalcitonin, sputum MC&S, GeneXpert, blood cultures, urine antigen tests, and urine LAM.
	•	Management: Empiric antibiotic selection based on severity, age, co-morbidities, and previous antibiotic exposure; WHO clinical case definition and empiric management for PCP; indication for corticosteroids in severe CAP; switching from IV to oral therapy and clinical stability criteria; evaluation of non-responding pneumonia.
	•	Complications & Prevention: Parapneumonic effusions, empyema, lung abscesses, acute cardiovascular events, long-term dysregulation/mortality; pneumococcal (PCV13, PPSV23) and influenza vaccination schedules.
	•	Bronchiectasis:
	•	Etiology: Post-TB damage, congenital causes (Cystic Fibrosis, Kartagener syndrome, primary ciliary dyskinesia), immunodeficiencies, and endobronchial obstruction.
	•	Clinical Features: Recurrent profuse purulent/foul-smelling sputum, hemoptysis, finger clubbing, and coarse inspiratory crackles.
	•	Investigations & Imaging: High-Resolution CT (HRCT) as the diagnostic gold standard, signet-ring sign, and tram-track lines.
	•	Management: Airway clearance techniques, mucolytics, targeted antibiotics, bronchodilators, and surgical resection indications.
	•	Asthma:
	•	Pathophysiology & Triggers: Atopic vs. non-atopic mechanisms, airway hyperresponsiveness, allergens, infections, cold air, and exercise.
	•	Diagnosis & Lung Function: Spirometry demonstrating reversibility (FEV1 improvement >12% post-bronchodilator), FEV1/FVC <70%, and Peak Expiratory Flow Rate (PEFR) variability.
	•	Pharmacotherapy: Stepwise management using short-acting beta-agonists (SABA), inhaled corticosteroids (ICS), and long-acting beta-agonists (LABA).
	•	Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD):
	•	Risk Factors & Clinical Features: Smoking history (pack-years), occupational exposures, barrel chest, hyperresonant percussion, Hoover's sign, and wheezing.
	•	Diagnosis & Management: Fixed airflow limitation on spirometry (FEV1/FVC <70%), CXR findings (hyperinflation, flattened diaphragms), stepwise pharmacological therapy, smoking cessation, and influenza/pneumococcal vaccination.
	•	Complications: Cor pulmonale and Type 2 respiratory failure.
	•	Pleural Effusion & Empyema:
	•	Differentiation: Light’s criteria to distinguish exudates from transudates.
	•	Etiologies: Exudative (pneumonia, TB, malignancy, pulmonary embolism, connective tissue disease) vs. Transudative (heart failure, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome).
	•	Empyema Pathophysiology: Exudative, fibropurulent, and organizing stages; pleural fluid characteristics (low pH <7.2, low glucose, high LDH); intercostal tube drainage and decortication.
	•	Interstitial Lung Disease (ILD) & Occupational Diseases:
	•	Etiologies: Pneumoconioses (asbestosis, silicosis), hypersensitivity pneumonitis, drug-induced ILD, and connective tissue disease-associated ILD.
	•	Clinical & Spirometric Pattern: Exertional dyspnea, fine basal crackles, and a restrictive deficit.
	•	Lung Abscess & Suppurative Lung Syndromes:
	•	Etiology & Pathogenesis: Aspiration (alcohol abuse, altered consciousness, bulbar palsy), dental/periodontal disease, septic emboli (IV drug use, infective endocarditis), and bronchial obstruction.
	•	Imaging & Treatment: CXR/CT showing cavitary lesions with air-fluid levels; anaerobic antibiotic coverage (clindamycin, metronidazole), postural drainage, and surgical indications.
	•	Lung Cancer / Bronchial Carcinoma:
	•	Histological Types: Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer (NSCLC: Adenocarcinoma, Squamous Cell, Large Cell) vs. Small Cell Lung Cancer (SCLC).
	•	Risk Factors: Cigarette smoking, secondhand smoke, radon, asbestos, HIV infection, and chronic scar tissue.
	•	Clinical Presentation & Local Invasion: Chronic cough, hemoptysis, Pancoast tumor (superior sulcus mass with Horner syndrome), Superior Vena Cava (SVC) obstruction, and recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy.
	•	Paraneoplastic Syndromes: Hypercalcemia (PTHrP from squamous cell), SIADH, Cushing syndrome (ectopic ACTH), Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome, and hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy.
	•	Staging & Therapeutics: TNM classification, staging CT/PET-CT, targeted therapy (EGFR, ALK, ROS1 mutations), immunotherapy, chemo-radiation, and surgical candidacy.
	•	General Respiratory Concepts:
	•	Respiratory Failure: Type 1 (hypoxemic) vs. Type 2 (hypercapnic) features and causes.
	•	Clinical Signs: Diagnostic approach to dyspnea, hemoptysis, respiratory distress signs, clubbing causes, and hilar/paratracheal lymphadenopathy.

2. Cardiovascular Medicine
	•	Ischemic Heart Disease (IHD) & Acute Coronary Syndromes (ACS):
	•	ACS Spectrum: Unstable Angina, Non-ST-Elevation Myocardial Infarction (NSTEMI), and ST-Elevation Myocardial Infarction (STEMI).
	•	Risk Factors: Modifiable (hypertension, diabetes, dyslipidemia, smoking, obesity) vs. non-modifiable (age, male sex, family history).
	•	Diagnosis: Differential diagnosis of acute chest pain, ECG changes (ST elevation, T wave inversion, ST depression), and cardiac biomarkers.
	•	Emergency Management & Complications: MONA-B protocol; complications including fatal/non-fatal arrhythmias, heart failure, papillary muscle rupture, ventricular aneurysm, and Dressler syndrome.
	•	Infective Endocarditis (IE):
	•	Classification & Pathogenesis: Native valve, prosthetic valve, nosocomial, and IV drug use-associated IE; acute (S. aureus) vs. subacute (Viridans streptococci).
	•	Clinical Features: Murmurs, cardiac failure, embolic/vascular phenomena (Janeway lesions, septic infarcts, mycotic aneurysms), and immunological phenomena (Osler nodes, Roth spots, glomerulonephritis, RF).
	•	Diagnostic Criteria: Modified Duke Criteria (major vs. minor criteria).
	•	Investigations & Prophylaxis: Transthoracic (TTE) vs. Transesophageal (TEE) echocardiography; restricted antibiotic prophylaxis guidelines for high-risk dental procedures.
	•	Acute Rheumatic Fever (ARF) & Rheumatic Heart Disease (RHD):
	•	Pathogenesis: Delayed immune response to Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcal pharyngitis.
	•	Diagnosis: Revised Jones Criteria (Major: Carditis, Polyarthritis, Chorea, Erythema marginatum, Subcutaneous nodules; Minor: Fever, Arthralgia, elevated ESR/CRP, prolonged PR interval; plus supporting strep evidence).
	•	Management & Prevention: Anti-inflammatory therapy (aspirin, prednisone); primary prevention (benzathine penicillin G) and secondary prophylaxis duration guidelines based on carditis/valvular damage severity.
	•	Hypertension:
	•	Definition & Diagnostic Rules: Diagnostic criteria (3 persistently elevated readings across at least 2 days).
	•	Etiology: Essential vs. secondary hypertension (renal artery stenosis, parenchymal renal disease, Cushing syndrome, Conn syndrome, pheochromocytoma, coarctation of aorta).
	•	Target Organ Damage & Complications: Hypertensive emergency criteria; microvascular (retinopathy, nephropathy, neuropathy) and macrovascular complications (IHD, CVA, PVD).
	•	Fundoscopy Grading: Grade 1 (silver wiring) to Grade 4 (papilledema).
	•	Pharmacotherapy: Thiazide diuretics, ACE inhibitors/ARBs, Calcium Channel Blockers.
	•	Valvular Heart Disease:
	•	Aortic Stenosis: Ejection systolic murmur in aortic area radiating to carotids, ejection click, heaving/pressure-loaded apex beat, parvus et tardus pulse.
	•	Mitral Regurgitation: Pansystolic murmur loudest at apex radiating to axilla, loud S3, volume-loaded apex beat, orthopnea.
	•	Mitral Stenosis & Aortic Regurgitation: Irregular pulse in MS; parvus et tardus / bounding pulse characteristics.
	•	Heart Failure & Arrhythmias:
	•	Heart Failure: Signs of left-sided vs. right-sided heart failure; NYHA functional classification; pharmacotherapy (ACE-I, beta-blockers, loop diuretics, spironolactone).
	•	Atrial Fibrillation (AF): Irregularly irregular pulse, pulse deficit, ECG findings (absent P waves, irregular R-R intervals), etiologies, and thromboembolic risk/anticoagulation rationale.
	•	Tachyarrhythmias: Broad classification of narrow vs. wide and regular vs. irregular tachycardias.
	•	Pericardial Disease: Pericardial tamponade clinical presentation, Beck's triad, and pericarditis.

3. Gastroenterology & Hepatology
	•	Peptic Ulcer Disease (PUD) & GERD:
	•	PUD Etiology & Risk Factors: Helicobacter pylori infection, NSAID use, alcohol, smoking, and stress.
	•	ALARM Features: Anaemia, Loss of weight, Anorexia, Recent onset, Melaena, and Swallowing difficulty (dysphagia) warranting urgent upper GI endoscopy.
	•	Management: PPIs, H2-receptor antagonists, H. pylori eradication triple therapy (PPI + Amoxicillin + Clarithromycin), and endoscopic hemostasis.
	•	GERD: Pathophysiology, heartburn, acid regurgitation, non-pharmacological measures, and complications (esophagitis, strictures).
	•	Gastrointestinal Bleeding:
	•	Upper GI Bleed: Presenting features (bright red haematemesis, melaena, coffee-ground vomitus); major causes (PUD, esophageal/gastric varices, Mallory-Weiss tears, erosive gastritis).
	•	Lower GI Bleed: Presenting features (haematochezia); common causes (diverticular disease, angiodysplasia, colonic carcinoma, hemorrhoids, IBD).
	•	Chronic Liver Disease (CLD) & Cirrhosis:
	•	Etiology: Alcohol abuse, chronic Hepatitis B & C, NAFLD/NASH, autoimmune hepatitis, primary biliary cholangitis, Wilson's disease, alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, hemachromatosis, and Schistosomiasis.
	•	Physical Stigmata: Palmar erythema, spider naevi, clubbing, leukonychia, Dupuytren contracture, gynecomastia, testicular atrophy, and parotid enlargement.
	•	Hepatic Decompensation & Failure: Jaundice, asterixis (flapping tremor), fetor hepaticus, constructional apraxia, hepatic encephalopathy precipitants (GI bleed, infection, electrolyte imbalance, sedatives).
	•	Diagnostic Evaluation: LFT patterns (hepatitic vs. cholestatic vs. mixed), albumin, prothrombin time/INR, Child-Pugh and MELD scoring systems, liver ultrasound, and biopsy.
	•	Portal Hypertension & Ascites:
	•	Portal Hypertension: Features (splenomegaly, esophageal/gastric varices, caput medusae, ascites); pre-hepatic, hepatic, and post-hepatic etiologies.
	•	Ascites Evaluation: Diagnostic paracentesis; Serum-Ascites Albumin Gradient (SAAG) cutoff interpretation (SAAG ≥11 g/L indicates portal hypertension vs. <11 g/L for non-portal causes like peritoneal TB, nephrotic syndrome, malignancy).
	•	Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis (SBP): Presentation (fever, abdominal pain, altered mental status), ascitic fluid neutrophil count >250 cells/µL, common pathogens (E. coli, Klebsiella), and antibiotic management.
	•	Pancreatic Disorders:
	•	Acute Pancreatitis: Etiologies (gallstones, alcohol bingeing, hyperlipidemia, drugs/ARVs, ERCP, hypercalcemia); presentation (epigastric pain radiating to back); diagnostic laboratory markers (raised serum lipase and amylase); radiological signs (colon cut-off sign, CT severity assessment); immediate and long-term complications.
	•	Chronic Pancreatitis: Irreversible exocrine and endocrine dysfunction; clinical triad (epigastric pain relieved sitting forward, steatorrhea, diabetes); diagnostic tests (CT calcifications, fecal elastase).
	•	Viral Hepatitis & Other GIT Conditions:
	•	Viral Hepatitis: Acute vs. chronic viral hepatitis (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, E); interpretation of Hep B serological markers (HBsAg, HBeAg, Anti-HBs, Anti-HBc IgM/IgG).
	•	Diarrhoea: Acute (<2 weeks) vs. chronic diarrhoea; causes of dysentery/bloody stools (Shigella, Salmonella, E. coli, Campylobacter, Entamoeba histolytica).
	•	Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD): Ulcerative Colitis (mucosal, continuous from rectum, crypt abscesses) vs. Crohn's Disease (transmural, skip lesions, any part of GIT, non-caseating granulomas).
	•	Symptom Approaches: Non-neoplastic vs. neoplastic causes of dysphagia; splenomegaly clinical features and classification; non-serious vs. serious causes of nausea and vomiting.

4. Neurology
	•	Cerebrovascular Accident (CVA / Stroke):
	•	Classification & Risk Factors: Ischaemic vs. haemorrhagic stroke; modifiable and non-modifiable risk factors.
	•	Localization: Vascular territory localization (Middle Cerebral Artery: upper extremity and facial weakness > lower extremity, aphasia vs. Anterior Cerebral Artery vs. Internal Capsule).
	•	Young Stroke: Differential diagnosis for stroke in young patients (vasculitis, antiphospholipid syndrome, thrombophilia, cardiac emboli/AF, SAH, venous sinus thrombosis).
	•	Meningitis & Central Nervous System Infections:
	•	Acute Bacterial Meningitis: Pathogens by age group (S. pneumoniae, N. meningitidis, H. influenzae, Listeria monocytogenes, Staph. aureus, Group B Strep); signs of meningism (neck stiffness, Kernig sign, Brudzinski sign); CSF analysis (turbid, neutrophilic pleocytosis, low glucose <1.9 mmol/L, high protein >2.2 g/L); empirical antibiotic cover and dexamethasone co-administration.
	•	Tuberculous Meningitis (TBM): Pathogenesis (Rich focus rupture into subarachnoid space), clinical staging, CSF characteristics (lymphocytic, high protein, low glucose), GeneXpert Ultra diagnostic yield, and prolonged anti-TB regimen + steroids.
	•	Cryptococcal Meningitis (CCM): Opportunistic encapsulated fungal infection in HIV patients; visual loss / raised ICP mechanism; diagnosis (India ink, CSF CrAg titre, culture); induction therapy (Amphotericin B + Flucytosine/Fluconazole), consolidation, and maintenance.
	•	Aseptic & Chronic Meningitis: Viral, fungal, parasitic, neoplastic, and drug-induced etiologies.
	•	Neuro-HIV:
	•	Intracranial Space-Occupying Lesions (SOL): Differential diagnosis in HIV patients (Toxoplasmosis, CNS Tuberculosis / Tuberculoma, Primary CNS Lymphoma).
	•	Imaging & Diagnostic Rationale: Ring-enhancing lesions on CT/MRI, CD4 threshold considerations (<200 cells/µL for Toxo/PCNSL), and empiric treatment trials.
	•	Other HIV Complications: HIV encephalopathy, cryptococcal meningitis, and ARV-induced neuro-side effects (efavirenz neuropsychiatric effects, INH neuropathy).
	•	Subarachnoid Haemorrhage (SAH):
	•	Etiology & Features: Ruptured berry aneurysm of circle of Willis, AV malformations; acute excruciating "thunderclap" headache, neck stiffness, focal neurology (CN III palsy).
	•	Diagnosis & Management: Non-contrast urgent head CT, lumbar puncture (xanthochromia if CT negative >12h); nimodipine for vasospasm prevention, endovascular coiling or surgical clipping.
	•	Seizures & Epilepsy:
	•	Classification & Causes: Focal vs. generalized seizures; triggers and underlying etiologies (trauma, stroke, infection, metabolic/hypoglycemia, alcohol/drug withdrawal).
	•	Evaluation & Therapeutics: Workup in HIV patients (exclude mass lesions, meningitis); first-line antiepileptic agents (carbamazepine, sodium valproate).
	•	Neurodegenerative & Movement Disorders:
	•	Parkinson’s Disease: Cardinal clinical triad (resting "pill-rolling" tremor, hypertonia/rigidity, bradykinesia); first-line symptomatic therapy (Levodopa/Carbidopa).
	•	Dementia vs. Delirium: Dementia types (Alzheimer’s, Vascular, Lewy Body) and screening (Mini-Mental State Examination) vs. Delirium (acute onset, fluctuating course, underlying precipitating illness); reversible causes of cognitive decline (depression, B12 deficiency, hypothyroidism, neurosyphilis).
	•	Neuromuscular & Peripheral Nerves:
	•	Myasthenia Gravis: Autoimmune neuromuscular junction disorder; ptosis, fatigable muscle weakness (curtain sign, peak sign, voice fatigability); myasthenic crisis.
	•	Peripheral Neuropathies: Proprioceptive loss, "glove-and-stocking" sensory loss, Romberg's test; Vitamin B12 deficiency myelopathy and neuropathy.

5. Rheumatology & Musculoskeletal Medicine
	•	Rheumatoid Arthritis (RA):
	•	Clinical Presentation: Chronic, symmetrical inflammatory polyarthritis of small joints of hands and feet (MCP, PIP joints; sparing DIPs), prolonged morning stiffness >1 hour.
	•	Deformities: Hand deformities (ulnar deviation, volar subluxation of MCPs, Z-deformity of thumb, swan-neck, boutonnière) and foot deformities (hallux valgus, clawing of toes).
	•	Diagnostics: Serological markers (Rheumatoid Factor, Anti-Cyclic Citrullinated Peptide / Anti-CCP antibodies); X-ray findings (soft tissue swelling, marginal erosions, joint space narrowing, juxta-articular osteoporosis).
	•	Extra-Articular Manifestations & Management: Rheumatoid nodules, interstitial lung disease, pleural effusions, Caplan syndrome, Felty syndrome, Sjögren's syndrome, accelerated atherosclerosis; treatment with DMARDs (Methotrexate), NSAIDs, and biologics.
	•	Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE):
	•	Diagnostic Criteria: Malar/butterfly rash, discoid rash, photosensitivity, painless oral/nasopharyngeal ulcers, non-erosive arthritis, serositis (pericarditis, pleuritis), lupus nephritis, neuropsychiatric features, hematological cytopenias.
	•	Autoantibody Testing: Screen with ANA; specific markers Anti-dsDNA (correlates with renal involvement and disease activity) and Anti-Smith; low complement levels (C3, C4); direct Coombs test.
	•	Complications & Drug-Induced Lupus: Lupus nephritis evaluation (proteinuria, red cell casts); drug-induced lupus causes (Isoniazid, Hydralazine, Procainamide).
	•	Osteoarthritis (OA):
	•	Pathophysiology & Distribution: Degenerative breakdown of hyaline articular cartilage; primary involvement of weight-bearing joints (knees, hips) and hands (DIP joints / Heberden nodes, PIP joints / Bouchard nodes, thumb CMC).
	•	Radiological Features: "LOSS" mnemonic (Loss of joint space, Osteophytes, Subchondral sclerosis, Subchondral cysts).
	•	Gout:
	•	Pathogenesis & Presentation: Monosodium urate crystal deposition; acute monoarthritis typically affecting 1st metatarsophalangeal joint (podagra), ankle, or knee.
	•	Risk Factors & Features: Hyperuricemia, male sex, obesity, high-purine diet, alcohol, hypertension, renal disease; chronic tophi development.
	•	Inflammatory Myositis & Scleroderma:
	•	Idiopathic Inflammatory Myositis: Dermatomyositis and Polymyositis; symmetrical proximal muscle weakness; muscle enzyme elevation (CK, AST, ALT); cutaneous signs (heliotrope rash, Gottron papules, V-neck sign, shawl sign, mechanic’s hands).
	•	Systemic Sclerosis / Scleroderma: Cutaneous and visceral fibrosis; Raynaud's phenomenon (triphasic color changes: white-blue-red), telangiectasia, scleroderma renal crisis, pulmonary hypertension, and CREST syndrome.
	•	Osteoporosis: Post-menopausal estrogen loss mechanism, dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DEXA) T-score < -2.5, bisphosphonate and calcium/vitamin D management.

6. Nephrology & Renal Medicine
	•	Acute Kidney Injury (AKI): Staging by serum creatinine and urine output; pre-renal azotemia, intrinsic renal causes (acute tubular necrosis, acute interstitial nephritis, glomerulonephritis), and post-renal urinary tract obstruction.
	•	Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD):
	•	Definition & Staging: GFR-based staging (Stage 1 to Stage 5 / End-Stage Renal Disease GFR <15 mL/min).
	•	Etiology & Features: Hypertension, Diabetes Mellitus, HIV-associated nephropathy (HIVAN), NSAID overuse, chronic pyelonephritis.
	•	Indications for Renal Replacement Therapy (RRT): Intractable hyperkalemia, persistent fluid overload / pulmonary edema, refractory metabolic acidosis, uremic pericarditis, or symptomatic uremia.
	•	Nephrotic vs. Nephritic Syndrome:
	•	Nephrotic Syndrome: Heavy proteinuria (>3.5 g/24h), hypoalbuminemia, generalized peripheral/periorbital edema, hyperlipidemia; minimal change disease, focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS), diabetic nephropathy.
	•	Nephritic Syndrome: Hematuria ("cola-colored" urine), dysmorphic RBCs/red cell casts, hypertension, oliguria, mild-to-moderate proteinuria; post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, IgA nephropathy, lupus nephritis.
	•	Urinary Tract Infections (UTIs): Uncomplicated lower UTI vs. complicated upper UTI (pyelonephritis); antibiotic selection (nitrofurantoin, trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, fluoroquinolones, ciprofloxacin).

7. Endocrinology & Metabolic Disorders
	•	Diabetes Mellitus (DM):
	•	Classification: Type 1 DM (autoimmune beta-cell destruction, insulin deficiency, younger onset, weight loss) vs. Type 2 DM (progressive insulin resistance, obesity, adult onset).
	•	Diagnostic Criteria: Fasting plasma glucose ≥7.0 mmol/L, random glucose / 2-hour OGTT ≥11.1 mmol/L, or HbA1c ≥6.5%.
	•	Complications: Microvascular (diabetic retinopathy, nephropathy, peripheral/autonomic neuropathy) and macrovascular (IHD, CVA, peripheral vascular disease).
	•	Pharmacotherapy: Insulin regimens for T1DM; Metformin as first-line for T2DM.
	•	Diabetic Emergencies:
	•	Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA): Precipitated by infection or insulin omission; hyperglycemia, ketonemia/ketonuria, metabolic acidosis, Kussmaul breathing, ketotic breath, hyperkalemia risk.
	•	Hyperosmolar Hyperglycemic State (HHS): Marked hyperglycemia, severe dehydration, high serum osmolality without significant ketoacidosis.
	•	Management: Aggressive IV fluid resuscitation, intravenous insulin, potassium monitoring and replacement, and treatment of underlying precipitants.
	•	Thyroid Disorders:
	•	Graves' Disease / Hyperthyroidism: Diffuse goiter with bruit, exophthalmos/proptosis, heat intolerance, weight loss, fine tremor, Plummer's nails, TSH receptor antibodies; anti-thyroid drugs, surgery.
	•	Hypothyroidism: Post-surgical / post-ablative, primary autoimmune; weight gain, cold intolerance, constipation, dry skin, brittle hair/nails, carpal tunnel syndrome, delayed reflexes; T4 (levothyroxine) replacement.
	•	Adrenal & Pituitary Disease:
	•	Cushing Syndrome: Glucocorticoid excess; moon face, central obesity, purple striae, proximal muscle weakness, hypertension, easy bruising.
	•	Addison Disease: Primary adrenal insufficiency; fatigue, weight loss, hyperpigmentation, hypotension, hyponatremia, hyperkalemia.

8. Haematology
	•	Anaemia:
	•	Iron Deficiency Anaemia: Microcytic, hypochromic anaemia; causes (chronic GI bleeding, menorrhagia, malabsorption, poor diet); clinical signs (pallor, koilonychia / spoon nails, angular cheilosis, atrophic glossitis); iron studies (low serum iron, low ferritin, high TIBC); oral iron replacement.
	•	Vitamin B12 & Folate Deficiency Anaemia: Macrocytic anaemia, hypersegmented neutrophils; megaloblastic bone marrow changes; associated neurological manifestations (subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord).
	•	Haemolytic Anaemia: Intravascular vs. extravascular haemolysis; auto-antibody-mediated (positive Direct Coombs test); clinical features (pallor, unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia/jaundice); elevated LDH, reduced haptoglobin, spherocytes/schistocytes on blood film.

9. Infectious Diseases & Antibiotic Stewardship
	•	Tuberculosis (TB):
	•	Pulmonary & Extrapulmonary TB: Pulmonary signs, night sweats, weight loss, hemoptysis; tuberculous lymphadenopathy, pleural TB, peritoneal/visceral TB, CNS TB/TBM.
	•	Diagnostics: Sputum smear microscopy (ZN stain), GeneXpert MTB/RIF (cartridge-based NAAT), mycobacterial culture, urine LAM assay in advanced HIV.
	•	Treatment: Standard RIFAFOUR regimen (2 months Rifampicin, Isoniazid, Pyrazinamide, Ethambutol + 4 months Rifampicin, Isoniazid); risk factors and global burden of Drug-Resistant TB (MDR-TB, XDR-TB).
	•	Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV):
	•	Staging & Diagnostics: WHO Clinical Staging (Stage 1 asymptomatic to Stage 4 AIDS-defining conditions like PCP, cryptococcal meningitis, Kaposi sarcoma); screening (ELISA/rapid tests) and monitoring (CD4 count, viral load).
	•	Antiretroviral Therapy (ART): Drug classes (NRTIs, NNRTIs, Protease Inhibitors, Integrase Inhibitors); fixed-dose combination regimens (e.g., Atripla: Tenofovir, Emtricitabine/Lamivudine, Efavirenz).
	•	Opportunistic Infections: PCP management, Cryptococcal Antigen (CrAg) screening and meningitis management, CNS Toxoplasmosis.
	•	Malaria & Parasitic Infections:
	•	Malaria: Plasmodium falciparum transmitted by Anopheles mosquito; clinical features, complications (cerebral malaria, severe anaemia, AKI, pulmonary edema); blood smear / rapid diagnostic test (HRP2).
	•	Schistosomiasis & Helminths: Schistosomiasis causing liver fibrosis, portal hypertension, and ascites.
	•	Sexually Transmitted Infections:
	•	Syphilis: Treponema pallidum; primary (painless chancre), secondary (diffuse rash, condyloma lata), tertiary (gumma, neurosyphilis); serology (RPR/VDRL, TPHA/FTA-ABS); Benzathine Penicillin G treatment.
	•	Optimal Antibiotic Use & Antibiotic Stewardship:
	•	Principles: Definition of optimal use (maximizing clinical cure while minimizing toxicity and resistance).
	•	Modes of Therapy: Empiric therapy vs. targeted/directed therapy vs. chemoprophylaxis (surgical and medical).
	•	Pharmacodynamics & Pharmacokinetics: Time-dependent (e.g., beta-lactams) vs. concentration-dependent antibiotics (e.g., aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones).
	•	Resistance Patterns: Hospital-acquired vs. community-acquired infections; ESCAPE pathogens (Enterococcus, Staph. aureus / MRSA, Klebsiella, Acinetobacter, Pseudomonas, Enterobacter); ESBL producers, carbapenem-resistant Enterobacteriaceae (CRE).
	•	Stewardship Strategies: Antibiotic timeout (48–72h reassessment), IV-to-oral switching criteria, avoiding unnecessary combination therapy, and antimicrobial stewardship teams.

9. Dermatology & Skin Disorders
	•	Bacterial Skin Infections:
	•	Impetigo: Superficial infection (Staphylococcus aureus and Group A Streptococcus pyogenes); non-bullous (honey-coloured crusted plaques around the nose and mouth) vs. bullous impetigo (flaccid blisters from exfoliatin toxin-producing Staph); complications including post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis; treatment with topical mupirocin or systemic flucloxacillin.
	•	Erysipelas & Cellulitis: Dermal (erysipelas, well-defined) vs. subcutaneous (cellulitis, ill-defined) infections; portal of entry identification; systemic constitutional symptoms; IV/oral cloxacillin/flucloxacillin management and limb elevation.
	•	Necrotising Fasciitis: Severe subcutaneous involvement, disproportionate pain, and surgical debridement/emergency referral.
	•	Furuncles, Carbuncles & Abscesses: Staphylococcal hair follicle/dermal infections; incision and drainage (I&D) and warm compresses.
	•	Viral Skin Infections:
	•	Herpes Simplex Virus (HSV): HSV-1 (orolabial) and HSV-2 (genital); painful grouped vesicles on an erythematous base evolving into shallow ulcers; trigeminal/sacral ganglion latency; Tzanck smear multinucleated giant cells; acyclovir management and eczema herpeticum complication in atopic dermatitis.
	•	Varicella-Zoster Virus (VZV):
	•	Varicella (Chickenpox): Inhaled droplet transmission; widespread "dewdrop on a rose petal" active/healing vesicles in all stages; complications in adults (pneumonitis, encephalitis) and Reye’s syndrome with aspirin.
	•	Herpes Zoster (Shingles): Reactivation from dorsal root ganglia in a unilateral dermatomal distribution; Hutchinson’s sign (nasociliary branch of V1 for ophthalmic zoster); postherpetic neuralgia and Ramsay Hunt syndrome (geniculate ganglion/facial nerve); early acyclovir window (<72h).
	•	Human Papillomavirus (HPV):
	•	Verrucae: Verruca vulgaris (HPV 2, 4); Plantar warts / myrmecia (HPV 1); Verruca planae (plane warts with Koebner phenomenon); thrombosed capillary punctate "specks".
	•	Anogenital Warts: Condylomata acuminata (low-risk HPV 6, 11) vs. high-risk oncogenic types (HPV 16, 18, 31, 33) linked to dysplasia/cervical cancer; topical keratinolytics, imiquimod, and cryotherapy.
	•	Molluscum Contagiosum: Poxvirus infection; pearly dome-shaped papules with central umbilication and cheesy keratin core; Henderson–Patterson inclusion bodies; self-limiting course in immunocompetent children vs. widespread presentation in HIV.
	•	Parasitic Infestations:
	•	Scabies: Sarcoptes scabiei mite infestation; intense nocturnal pruritus; superficial linear burrows in web spaces, wrists, and groin (sparing head/nicely); crusted (Norwegian) scabies in immunocompromised patients; treatment with 25% benzyl benzoate or 5% permethrin alongside comprehensive linen decontamination and simultaneous household contact treatment.
	•	Fungal & Yeast Skin Infections:
	•	Superficial Dermatophyte Infections (Tinea): Caused by Trichophyton, Microsporum, and Epidermophyton; diagnostic KOH mount ("spaghetti and meatballs" or hyphae/spores); specific clinical types:
	•	Tinea Corporis: Annular scaly plaques with central clearing and active raised borders.
	•	Tinea Cruris: Groin plaque sparing the scrotum.
	•	Tinea Pedis: Interdigital scaling/maceration (4th web space).
	•	Tinea Capitis: Scaly patches, non-cicatricial alopecia, broken hairs, and inflammatory kerion; mandatory systemic therapy (griseofulvin/fluconazole) as topicals cannot penetrate hair shafts.
	•	Onychomycosis (Tinea Unguium): Yellow-brown discoloration, subungual hyperkeratosis, and crumbling; terbinafine or pulse-dose itraconazole.
	•	Candidiasis: Candida albicans infections; intertriginous candidiasis with characteristic peripheral satellite pustules; oral thrush (scrapable white plaques); diaper dermatitis; topical or systemic azoles.
	•	Pityriasis (Tinea) Versicolor: Malassezia furfur overgrowth in hot/humid climates; asymptomatic hypo- or hyperpigmented macules on the trunk and back due to azelaic acid inhibition of melanin synthesis.
	•	Acneiform Eruptions & Rosacea:
	•	Acne Vulgaris: Inflammatory pilosebaceous unit disorder driven by hyperkeratinization, excess sebum (5-alpha reductase), Cutibacterium acnes colonization, and inflammation; comedones (open/closed), papules, pustules, nodules, cysts, and scarring (icepick, boxcar, rolling); stepwise management using topical retinoids, benzoyl peroxide, topical/oral antibiotics (doxycycline), hormonal therapy (COCs with anti-androgens), and systemic isotretinoin with strict teratogenicity/pregnancy prevention counseling.
	•	Perioral Dermatitis: Symmetrical micropapules around the mouth/nose linked to topical steroid use, featuring a characteristic sparing rim around the vermillion border.
	•	Rosacea: Central facial inflammatory papules and pustules (forehead, nose, cheeks, chin) triggered by heat, alcohol, and sunlight.
	•	Dermatitis & Eczema:
	•	Atopic Dermatitis (Eczema): T-cell mediated process with epidermal barrier dysfunction linked to filaggrin gene mutations; acute (weepy vesicles), subacute (scaling), and chronic (lichenification) phases; age-specific distribution (infants: face/scalp; children/adults: flexural surfaces); associated atopic features (Dennie-Morgan folds, hyperlinear palms); step-up management using emollients, topical corticosteroids, calcineurin inhibitors, and systemic immunosuppressants (cyclosporine, methotrexate).
	•	Seborrheic Dermatitis: Greasy yellowish scales in areas rich in sebaceous glands (scalp "cradle cap", face, nasolabial folds, axilla, groin); strong association with Malassezia and HIV infection; differentiation from atopic dermatitis.
	•	Contact Dermatitis: Irritant vs. allergic contact dermatitis (nickel, cement); patch testing and avoidance.
	•	Stasis Eczema: Chronic venous insufficiency, lower limb lipodermatosclerosis, and champagne-bottle tapering.
	•	Papulosquamous & Autoimmune Conditions:
	•	Lichen Planus: Autoimmune reaction; the "5 Ps" (Pruritic, Polygonal, Purple/violaceous, Plane-topped, Papules/plaques); pathognomonic Wickham’s striae; nail changes (pterygium, pitting) and oral ulcerations; drug-induced triggers (ACE inhibitors, thiazides, beta-blockers) and Koebner phenomenon.
	•	Pityriasis Rosea: Acute self-limiting eruption starting with a herald patch followed by oval scaly plaques aligned along Langer’s lines in a "Christmas tree" back distribution.
	•	Psoriasis: Inflammatory hyperproliferative disorder; well-circumscribed salmon-pink plaques with silvery-white scales and Auspitz sign (pinpoint bleeding upon scale removal); variants (Vulgaris, Inverse/flexural, Guttate post-strep, Erythrodermic, Pustular); nail pitting and oil-drop discoloration; systemic associations (psoriatic arthritis, metabolic syndrome/CVD risk); topical therapies, phototherapy, and systemic agents (methotrexate, acitretin, cyclosporine, biologics) with strict contraindication of systemic steroids.
	•	Vitiligo: Acquired autoimmune destruction of melanocytes resulting in well-demarcated asymptomatic white patches, poliosis, and Koebnerization; topical calcineurin inhibitors, narrow-band UVB, and depigmentation options.
	•	Malignant Skin Lesions:
	•	Basal Cell Carcinoma (BCC): Most common skin cancer; malignant proliferation of basal keratinocytes linked to UVB exposure; pearly pink/skin-colored nodule with rolled telangiectatic borders and central ulceration; locally aggressive with rare metastasis.
	•	Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC): Malignant neoplasm of keratinocytes; risk factors include UV radiation, chronic scars/ulcers, and HPV 16/18; indurated, scaly, crusted plaques or nodules with rapid enlargement.
	•	Malignant Melanoma: Malignant proliferation of melanocytes; evaluated using the ABCDE criteria (Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Colour variation, Diameter >6 mm, Evolving); primary locations on the back (men) and calves (women).

